1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,590
Hola. This is Liam, in for Bear. Claude can carry a lot of skills, but it doesn't

2
00:00:04,610 --> 00:00:09,201
read them all. So don't ask how Claude reads every skill. Ask how it knows which one

3
00:00:09,221 --> 00:00:09,743
to open.

4
00:00:09,813 --> 00:00:14,443
Three terms. A skill: a folder of instructions, scripts, and resources that Claude

5
00:00:14,463 --> 00:00:19,807
loads for a task. SKILL.md: its one required file, with a name and description on

6
00:00:19,827 --> 00:00:24,099
top and instructions below. And progressive disclosure: more detail loads only as

7
00:00:24,119 --> 00:00:25,530
the task needs it.

8
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:30,245
Picture your skills as a filing cabinet. Each drawer is one skill. And on the front

9
00:00:30,265 --> 00:00:31,994
of every drawer, there's a label.

10
00:00:32,064 --> 00:00:36,662
Pull one drawer out. Up front is the one file every skill needs: SKILL.md. Its top

11
00:00:36,682 --> 00:00:41,569
holds a name, and a description of what the skill does and when to use it. That's

12
00:00:41,589 --> 00:00:43,301
the label. Below it, the instructions.

13
00:00:43,371 --> 00:00:47,660
Behind it, three optional folders. Scripts, code Claude can run. References,

14
00:00:47,680 --> 00:00:51,578
documents it can read. And assets, like templates and images.

15
00:00:51,648 --> 00:00:56,222
Here's the trick. When a session starts, Claude doesn't open the cabinet. It reads

16
00:00:56,242 --> 00:01:00,487
only the labels: every skill's name and description. That's all that's always in

17
00:01:00,507 --> 00:01:00,815
context.

18
00:01:00,885 --> 00:01:06,511
Then a task arrives: fill in this PDF form. Claude checks it against the labels. The

19
00:01:06,531 --> 00:01:11,098
PDF skill matches, so only that drawer slides open, and its instructions load.

20
00:01:11,168 --> 00:01:15,743
Those instructions point to more files, and Claude pulls one only when the job needs

21
00:01:15,763 --> 00:01:20,645
it. Filling a form? Read the forms guide. A script can even run without its code

22
00:01:20,665 --> 00:01:22,789
being loaded. Only its output comes back.

23
00:01:22,859 --> 00:01:27,715
Every other drawer stays shut. So you can install many skills, and each one costs

24
00:01:27,735 --> 00:01:31,941
little more than its label until a task needs it. That's progressive disclosure.

25
00:01:32,011 --> 00:01:35,977
So the description does the real work. It's the label that decides whether the

26
00:01:35,997 --> 00:01:40,247
drawer opens. The spec's own bad example is: helps with PDFs. Say what the skill

27
00:01:40,267 --> 00:01:44,517
does, and when to use it, in the words people actually type. And Anthropic's skill

28
00:01:44,537 --> 00:01:48,218
guide says Claude tends to under-use skills, so make it a little pushy.

29
00:01:48,288 --> 00:01:52,244
Your turn. Paste this into Claude: Here's a task I repeat every week: [describe it

30
00:01:52,264 --> 00:01:56,220
in two sentences]. Draft a SKILL.md for it. Give it a short lowercase name with

31
00:01:56,240 --> 00:02:00,727
hyphens, and a description that says what it does and exactly when to use it, in the

32
00:02:00,747 --> 00:02:04,968
words I'd actually type. Then write the steps below, and tell me what belongs in a

33
00:02:04,988 --> 00:02:08,149
separate reference file or script. Then check two things yourself. Write three

34
00:02:08,169 --> 00:02:11,860
different ways you'd ask for this task. Does the description catch all three? And

35
00:02:11,880 --> 00:02:16,101
name one request that should not open it. Does the description stay out of the way?

36
00:02:16,171 --> 00:02:19,981
How a Skill Loads. At Nik Bear Brown.
